// #86 MISS 2 — THE RESIDENCY EXEMPTION, IN THE ABUSE DIRECTION.
// Runtime-confirmed use-after-free (the returned `str` stops comparing equal
// after 256 intervening String allocations).
//
// `type_is_residency_exempt` was a NAME test over `ts_.residency_exempt`,
// auto-populated for ANY struct with a field whose package-stripped name is
// `Rc`/`Arc`, and registered under the BARE name too. So the single field
// `h: Rc<i64>` below switched the WHOLE #86 escape gate off for `E` — both the
// return gate and the `let` provenance record — and returning `e.v`, a borrow
// of a fn-local `String`, admitted. MEASURED at the #86 landing: rc 0.
// The `Rc` share keeps a DIFFERENT allocation alive and says nothing about `v`.
//
// CTRL-D had proved the exemption NECESSARY (see
// pass/bc_esc_holder_residency_backed_admit, which is the shape
// examples/writ_container_showcase.logos `make_held_doc` actually needs) and
// nothing at all about what it let through — the case the repo's standing rule
// calls an unchecked hatch: worse than no gate, because the green vouches for it.
//
// THE RULE THAT SEPARATES THEM, and the ONE VARIABLE against the admit twin:
// WHERE THE ESCAPING BORROW IS ROOTED. In the admit twin every local the
// escape names IS the residency holder (`h: Rc<Writ>`); here it is `o`, a
// plain `String` no share was ever taken of.
//
// The one-variable CONTROL inside this hole: `h: Rc<i64>` → `h: i64` already
// flipped rc 0 → rc 1 at the #86 landing, which is what named the exemption
// as the cause rather than the borrow walk.
package bc_esc_holder_residency_abuse_dangle;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.rc;

pub struct E { pub h: Rc<i64>, pub v: str }

pub fn bad() -> str {
    let o: String = String::from("hello");
    let e: E = E { h: rc_new::<i64>(1i64), v: o.as_str() };
    return e.v;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    if bad().len() == 5i64 { return 0i32; }
    return 1i32;
}
